1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,174
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:05,174 --> 00:00:10,779
Science, Unit 2: Data and Binary. We'll
see how computers store numbers, text,

3
00:00:10,779 --> 00:00:13,653
images and sound, all as ones and zeros.

4
00:00:14,827 --> 00:00:17,536
You count with ten digits, zero through
nine.

5
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:20,696
A computer only has two. Zero and one.

6
00:00:21,146 --> 00:00:24,538
So how does a computer count past one?
Let's find out.

7
00:00:25,688 --> 00:00:28,717
Inside a computer are billions of tiny
switches.

8
00:00:29,167 --> 00:00:33,604
Each switch is either off or on. We write
off as zero, and on as one.

9
00:00:34,054 --> 00:00:38,300
A single zero or one is called a bit,
short for binary digit.

10
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:42,202
First, let's look at how our normal
numbers work.

11
00:00:42,652 --> 00:00:45,084
Take the number three hundred forty five.

12
00:00:45,534 --> 00:00:50,974
The five means five ones. The four means
four tens. The three means three hundreds.

13
00:00:51,424 --> 00:00:56,714
Each place is worth ten times the place to
its right. That's why we call it base ten.

14
00:00:57,864 --> 00:01:00,531
Binary works the same way, but with base
two.

15
00:01:00,981 --> 00:01:04,138
Each place is worth two times the place to
its right.

16
00:01:04,588 --> 00:01:10,525
So instead of ones, tens and hundreds, the
places are one, two, four, eight, sixteen,

17
00:01:10,525 --> 00:01:11,223
and so on.

18
00:01:12,873 --> 00:01:13,961
Now let's count.

19
00:01:14,411 --> 00:01:19,595
Zero. One. Now we're out of digits, so we
carry, just like going from nine to ten.

20
00:01:20,045 --> 00:01:24,994
One zero is two. One one is three. Carry
again: one zero zero is four.

21
00:01:25,444 --> 00:01:30,351
Five, six, seven. And carrying once more,
one zero zero zero is eight.

22
00:01:31,501 --> 00:01:35,234
To read a binary number, add up the places
that hold a one.

23
00:01:35,684 --> 00:01:37,391
Take one zero one one.

24
00:01:37,841 --> 00:01:41,510
There's a one in the eights place, the
twos place, and the ones place.

25
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:47,656
Eight plus two plus one is eleven. So one
zero one one in binary is eleven.

26
00:01:48,806 --> 00:01:52,049
Going the other way, start with the
biggest place that fits.

27
00:01:52,499 --> 00:01:55,678
Let's convert thirteen. Eight fits,
leaving five.

28
00:01:56,128 --> 00:02:01,568
Four fits, leaving one. Two doesn't fit,
so that place gets a zero. And one fits.

29
00:02:02,018 --> 00:02:04,450
So thirteen is one one zero one.

30
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:09,802
Computers usually group bits in eights.
Eight bits make one byte.

31
00:02:10,252 --> 00:02:15,095
With eight bits, the biggest number is all
ones, which is two hundred fifty five.

32
00:02:15,545 --> 00:02:19,257
Counting zero, that's two hundred fifty
six different values.

33
00:02:20,407 --> 00:02:24,524
Now you try. What is one zero zero one
zero in decimal?

34
00:02:24,974 --> 00:02:26,254
Pause the video now.

35
00:02:26,704 --> 00:02:29,350
The answer is eighteen: sixteen plus two.

36
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:35,230
Next, we'll see why programmers also use
hexadecimal, a shorter way to write

37
00:02:35,230 --> 00:02:35,730
binary.

38
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:41,595
Here's a byte in binary: one one zero one,
zero one one zero.

39
00:02:42,045 --> 00:02:44,626
Eight digits, and it's easy to lose your
place.

40
00:02:45,076 --> 00:02:49,961
Programmers use a shorter way to write the
same thing. It's called hexadecimal.

41
00:02:51,111 --> 00:02:54,973
Hexadecimal is base sixteen, so it needs
sixteen digits.

42
00:02:55,423 --> 00:02:57,599
Zero through nine give us ten of them.

43
00:02:58,049 --> 00:03:03,609
For the other six, we borrow letters. A is
ten, B is eleven, C is twelve, D is

44
00:03:03,609 --> 00:03:06,603
thirteen, E is fourteen, and F is fifteen.

45
00:03:07,753 --> 00:03:09,545
Here's why hex is so useful.

46
00:03:09,995 --> 00:03:15,798
Four bits can make exactly sixteen
patterns, from zero zero zero zero up to

47
00:03:15,798 --> 00:03:17,035
one one one one.

48
00:03:17,485 --> 00:03:23,224
That's the same as one hex digit. So every
group of four bits becomes one hex digit.

49
00:03:24,374 --> 00:03:27,851
Let's convert our byte. Split it into two
groups of four.

50
00:03:28,301 --> 00:03:34,061
One one zero one is eight plus four plus
one, which is thirteen. In hex, that's D.

51
00:03:34,511 --> 00:03:37,903
Zero one one zero is four plus two, which
is six.

52
00:03:38,353 --> 00:03:42,364
So the byte is D six in hex. Two
characters instead of eight.

53
00:03:43,514 --> 00:03:48,625
Hex has place values too, but each place
is worth sixteen times the one to its

54
00:03:48,625 --> 00:03:49,018
right.

55
00:03:49,468 --> 00:03:54,268
In D six, the D is in the sixteens place,
and the six is in the ones place.

56
00:03:54,718 --> 00:03:59,990
Thirteen times sixteen is two hundred
eight. Add six, and you get two hundred

57
00:03:59,990 --> 00:04:00,606
fourteen.

58
00:04:01,756 --> 00:04:03,761
You've probably seen hex already.

59
00:04:04,211 --> 00:04:10,991
Web colors are written in hex. Pound F F
eight eight zero zero means red F F, green

60
00:04:10,991 --> 00:04:13,278
eight eight, blue zero zero.

61
00:04:13,728 --> 00:04:19,275
F F is two hundred fifty five, the most
red possible. So this color is orange.

62
00:04:19,725 --> 00:04:25,564
Hex also shows up in memory addresses and
error codes, anywhere people need to read

63
00:04:25,564 --> 00:04:26,338
raw binary.

64
00:04:27,488 --> 00:04:32,587
Your turn. Convert one zero one zero, zero
zero one one to hex.

65
00:04:33,037 --> 00:04:34,317
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:34,767 --> 00:04:39,972
One zero one zero is ten, which is A. Zero
zero one one is three.

67
00:04:40,422 --> 00:04:45,521
So the answer is A three. Next, we'll see
how computers store text as numbers.

68
00:04:46,671 --> 00:04:50,276
A computer can only store numbers. Ones
and zeros.

69
00:04:50,726 --> 00:04:53,990
So when you type the letter A, what
actually gets saved?

70
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:56,787
Let's find out how text becomes numbers.

71
00:04:57,937 --> 00:05:01,713
The trick is an agreement. Every character
gets its own number.

72
00:05:02,163 --> 00:05:07,954
Capital A is sixty five. B is sixty six, C
is sixty seven, and so on through the

73
00:05:07,954 --> 00:05:08,606
alphabet.

74
00:05:09,056 --> 00:05:14,218
Lowercase letters start at ninety seven.
Even a space has a number: thirty two.

75
00:05:15,368 --> 00:05:18,611
Let's encode the word Hi, with an
exclamation mark.

76
00:05:19,061 --> 00:05:24,632
H is seventy two. Lowercase i is one
hundred five. The exclamation mark is

77
00:05:24,632 --> 00:05:25,610
thirty three.

78
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,898
In memory, those are just three bytes of
binary.

79
00:05:30,048 --> 00:05:34,421
This table is called ASCII. It was created
in the nineteen sixties.

80
00:05:34,871 --> 00:05:40,101
ASCII uses seven bits, so it has room for
one hundred twenty eight characters:

81
00:05:40,101 --> 00:05:44,258
English letters, digits, punctuation, and
a few control codes.

82
00:05:44,708 --> 00:05:50,215
Here's a surprise. The character seven is
stored as fifty five, not as the number

83
00:05:50,215 --> 00:05:53,070
seven. A digit you type is text, not math.

84
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:57,847
But one hundred twenty eight characters
isn't enough for the world.

85
00:05:58,297 --> 00:06:03,332
What about accented letters, Greek,
Russian, Japanese, Chinese, or emoji?

86
00:06:03,782 --> 00:06:08,951
Unicode solves this. It gives a number,
called a code point, to over one hundred

87
00:06:08,951 --> 00:06:12,763
fifty thousand characters from nearly
every writing system.

88
00:06:13,213 --> 00:06:17,394
A is still sixty five, so ASCII fits right
inside Unicode.

89
00:06:18,544 --> 00:06:22,171
To store Unicode, most of the web uses UTF
eight.

90
00:06:22,621 --> 00:06:25,970
In UTF eight, a character takes one to
four bytes.

91
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:32,235
Plain English letters still use one byte,
exactly like ASCII. Other characters use

92
00:06:32,235 --> 00:06:34,079
more. An emoji takes four.

93
00:06:35,229 --> 00:06:39,816
Your turn. Decode these numbers: sixty
seven, sixty five, eighty four.

94
00:06:40,266 --> 00:06:41,546
Pause the video now.

95
00:06:41,996 --> 00:06:47,286
Sixty seven is C, sixty five is A, and
eighty four is T. The word is cat.

96
00:06:47,736 --> 00:06:50,296
Next, we'll see how images become numbers.

97
00:06:51,446 --> 00:06:54,305
Every photo on your phone is stored as
numbers.

98
00:06:54,755 --> 00:06:59,405
But a picture isn't text, and it isn't a
single number. So how does that work?

99
00:06:59,855 --> 00:07:00,901
Let's zoom in.

100
00:07:02,051 --> 00:07:07,128
Zoom in far enough on any digital image,
and you'll see a grid of tiny squares.

101
00:07:07,578 --> 00:07:11,013
Each square is called a pixel, short for
picture element.

102
00:07:11,463 --> 00:07:17,095
A pixel shows exactly one color. The whole
image is just a grid of colored pixels.

103
00:07:18,245 --> 00:07:20,847
Let's start simple, with only black and
white.

104
00:07:21,297 --> 00:07:25,308
One bit per pixel is enough. Zero for
black, one for white.

105
00:07:25,758 --> 00:07:31,105
So this little eight by eight smiley face
is just sixty four bits, written row by

106
00:07:31,105 --> 00:07:31,369
row.

107
00:07:32,519 --> 00:07:35,975
For color, screens mix three lights: red,
green, and blue.

108
00:07:36,425 --> 00:07:41,568
Each one gets a brightness from zero to
two hundred fifty five, which fits in one

109
00:07:41,568 --> 00:07:41,886
byte.

110
00:07:42,336 --> 00:07:47,751
Full red with no green or blue is pure
red. Red and green together make yellow.

111
00:07:47,751 --> 00:07:49,739
All three at full make white.

112
00:07:50,889 --> 00:07:55,134
Three bytes per pixel gives over sixteen
million possible colors.

113
00:07:55,584 --> 00:08:00,832
That's two hundred fifty six, times two
hundred fifty six, times two hundred fifty

114
00:08:00,832 --> 00:08:01,088
six.

115
00:08:02,238 --> 00:08:03,753
Now let's count the cost.

116
00:08:04,203 --> 00:08:09,512
A typical phone photo is about four
thousand by three thousand pixels. That's

117
00:08:09,512 --> 00:08:11,029
twelve million pixels.

118
00:08:11,479 --> 00:08:16,609
At three bytes each, that's thirty six
million bytes, about thirty six megabytes,

119
00:08:16,609 --> 00:08:17,495
for one photo.

120
00:08:17,945 --> 00:08:21,913
That's why images are usually compressed,
which is coming up soon.

121
00:08:23,063 --> 00:08:27,927
Resolution is the number of pixels. More
pixels means finer detail.

122
00:08:28,377 --> 00:08:33,386
Color depth is the number of bits per
pixel. More bits means more possible

123
00:08:33,386 --> 00:08:33,860
colors.

124
00:08:34,310 --> 00:08:37,894
Both make images look better, and both
make files bigger.

125
00:08:39,044 --> 00:08:44,918
Your turn. A tiny image is ten pixels wide
and ten pixels tall, with three bytes per

126
00:08:44,918 --> 00:08:45,337
pixel.

127
00:08:45,787 --> 00:08:48,347
How many bytes is it? Pause the video now.

128
00:08:48,797 --> 00:08:54,216
Ten times ten is one hundred pixels. Times
three bytes is three hundred bytes.

129
00:08:54,666 --> 00:08:56,757
Next, we'll turn sound into numbers.

130
00:08:57,907 --> 00:09:02,814
Music, voices, this narration. A computer
stores all of it as numbers.

131
00:09:03,264 --> 00:09:07,957
But sound is a smooth wave in the air. How
do you turn a wave into numbers?

132
00:09:08,407 --> 00:09:09,836
The answer is sampling.

133
00:09:10,986 --> 00:09:17,325
Sound is a vibration. A microphone turns
it into a signal that rises and falls over

134
00:09:17,325 --> 00:09:17,706
time.

135
00:09:18,156 --> 00:09:23,319
This curve is smooth and continuous. It
has a value at every single instant.

136
00:09:23,769 --> 00:09:28,989
A computer can't store infinitely many
values, so it measures the wave at regular

137
00:09:28,989 --> 00:09:30,020
moments instead.

138
00:09:31,170 --> 00:09:35,910
Each measurement is called a sample. It
records the height of the wave at that

139
00:09:35,910 --> 00:09:36,396
instant.

140
00:09:36,846 --> 00:09:41,092
Store the samples in order, and you have
the sound as a list of numbers.

141
00:09:41,542 --> 00:09:45,510
To play it back, the computer rebuilds the
wave from those samples.

142
00:09:46,660 --> 00:09:49,348
How often you sample is called the sample
rate.

143
00:09:49,798 --> 00:09:55,024
Sample too slowly, and you miss the fast
wiggles. The wave you rebuild looks wrong.

144
00:09:55,474 --> 00:09:59,122
Sample faster, and the copy gets closer to
the original.

145
00:10:00,272 --> 00:10:05,584
Music on a CD uses forty four thousand one
hundred samples every second.

146
00:10:06,034 --> 00:10:09,768
That's fast enough to capture every pitch
most humans can hear.

147
00:10:10,918 --> 00:10:14,715
The second setting is bit depth: how many
bits store each sample.

148
00:10:15,165 --> 00:10:20,087
With only a few bits, each sample has to
snap to a few allowed levels, and the

149
00:10:20,087 --> 00:10:21,160
sound gets rough.

150
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:27,386
CD audio uses sixteen bits, which allows
sixty five thousand five hundred thirty

151
00:10:27,386 --> 00:10:28,180
six levels.

152
00:10:29,330 --> 00:10:32,104
So how big is one minute of CD audio?

153
00:10:32,554 --> 00:10:38,069
Forty four thousand one hundred samples,
times two bytes, times two channels for

154
00:10:38,069 --> 00:10:39,999
stereo, times sixty seconds.

155
00:10:40,449 --> 00:10:44,801
That's about ten and a half million bytes,
roughly ten megabytes per minute.

156
00:10:45,951 --> 00:10:51,350
Your turn. A recording uses ten thousand
samples per second, with one byte per

157
00:10:51,350 --> 00:10:52,458
sample, in mono.

158
00:10:52,908 --> 00:10:56,172
How many bytes for ten seconds? Pause the
video now.

159
00:10:56,622 --> 00:11:00,824
Ten thousand times one times ten is one
hundred thousand bytes.

160
00:11:01,274 --> 00:11:04,816
Next, we'll see how compression makes all
these files smaller.

161
00:11:05,965 --> 00:11:11,213
A single phone photo can take thirty six
megabytes. A minute of music, ten.

162
00:11:11,663 --> 00:11:15,290
Yet the files on your phone are usually
much smaller than that.

163
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:20,028
The trick is compression: storing the same
information in fewer bits.

164
00:11:21,178 --> 00:11:23,887
Compression works because data often
repeats.

165
00:11:24,337 --> 00:11:28,881
Look at this row of pixels: four white,
three black, two white, one black.

166
00:11:29,331 --> 00:11:34,195
Instead of storing every pixel, we can
store how many in a row, and what color.

167
00:11:34,645 --> 00:11:40,149
Four W, three B, two W, one B. That's
called run length encoding.

168
00:11:41,299 --> 00:11:45,310
The original took ten symbols. The
compressed version takes eight.

169
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:50,837
With long runs, like a clear blue sky in a
photo, the savings get much bigger.

170
00:11:51,287 --> 00:11:55,869
And nothing is lost. We can rebuild the
exact original from the compressed

171
00:11:55,869 --> 00:11:56,364
version.

172
00:11:57,514 --> 00:12:00,608
Compression where nothing is lost is
called lossless.

173
00:12:01,058 --> 00:12:04,023
Zip files and P N G images are lossless.

174
00:12:04,473 --> 00:12:09,839
Text, code, and spreadsheets must use
lossless compression. Change a single bit,

175
00:12:09,839 --> 00:12:12,388
and a number or a word could be wrong.

176
00:12:13,538 --> 00:12:18,896
The other kind is lossy compression. It
throws away detail you probably won't

177
00:12:18,896 --> 00:12:19,383
notice.

178
00:12:19,833 --> 00:12:26,003
A J P E G photo blends tiny color
differences together. An M P three removes

179
00:12:26,003 --> 00:12:28,601
sounds your ear can barely hear.

180
00:12:29,051 --> 00:12:33,936
Lossy files can be ten times smaller, but
the lost detail is gone for good.

181
00:12:35,086 --> 00:12:36,366
So which should you use?

182
00:12:36,816 --> 00:12:39,931
If you need the exact original back,
choose lossless.

183
00:12:40,381 --> 00:12:45,998
If you need the smallest file, and small
changes are fine, choose lossy. That's why

184
00:12:45,998 --> 00:12:48,232
streaming video and music use it.

185
00:12:49,382 --> 00:12:55,692
Your turn. Compress this row with run
length encoding: six W, then three B, then

186
00:12:55,692 --> 00:12:56,166
one W.

187
00:12:56,616 --> 00:12:57,896
Pause the video now.

188
00:12:58,346 --> 00:13:01,674
The answer is six W, three B, one W.

189
00:13:02,124 --> 00:13:06,518
That's the end of Unit two. Next, we'll
start Unit three: algorithms.

190
00:13:07,668 --> 00:13:12,345
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

191
00:13:12,345 --> 00:13:13,620
miss the next lesson.

192
00:13:14,070 --> 00:13:14,966
See you there.

